From 15a7f6303d673803f1a115d60d14c45269d2fd16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 16:50:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] Survive FUB's refusal to serve bulk text history, and
 act on nothing when it happens
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The nightly audit was dying before it produced anything:

  FUB GET /textMessages → 400: Please provide one of the following
  parameters: personId, threadId, phone, toNumber, fromNumber, ...

Follow Up Boss will not serve /v1/textMessages unscoped. That is a
permanent fact about the API, not an outage, so a crash was the wrong
response — but the obvious fix is worse than the crash.

A text counts as working a lead. Catching the 400 and carrying on with
`texts: []` leaves the touch index missing a whole channel for the whole
database, and every lead worked by text and not by phone reads as untouched.
The engine would then nudge the agents who text rather than call, stamp them
with customBattrAtRiskSince — which is precisely what satisfies the
warn-first interlock — and sweep their leads the following night. The
failure mode of a missing signal here is not doing less. It is doing more,
to the wrong people, with a paper trail that looks correct.

So the run continues and reports, and acts on nothing:

  - fub.activity() returns `textsUnavailable` carrying FUB's own message.
    Only 4xx degrades; 5xx and network failures still throw, because those
    might really be different tomorrow.
  - Nudges and sweeps are both held. Nudges are not the harmless half — a
    nudge is what arms tomorrow's sweep.
  - Agent digests are withheld. An inflated neglect count in a report is a
    bad number; the same count in thirty agents' inboxes over their own
    names is an accusation the engine cannot substantiate.
  - The report leads with the reason, above the counts, where it cannot be
    skimmed past.

This is the doctrine the reply check already followed: if the lookup fails,
the sweep is held and the report says so.

Why this reached production: the self-test's fixture server answered 200 to
every GET regardless of query string, so the bulk read FUB rejects outright
passed here and the suite went green. A stand-in more permissive than the
thing it stands in for does not test the client, it flatters it. It now
enforces the real scoping requirements for /textMessages and /emails, and
the end-to-end scenario runs twice — once with the text history served, once
refused.

Errors now carry .status/.endpoint/.detail so callers branch on the status
code rather than regexing the message.

battr:test: 148 checks, green. Against the pre-fix engine, 8 of the new
checks fail — starting with "the engine exits cleanly".

