From de6af5b29bcf99de6637101285d1e089295567d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 16:54:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/10] Recover the text channel one lead at a time when FUB
 refuses it in bulk
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The previous commit made the audit safe. It did not make it useful: Follow
Up Boss always refuses the bulk text read, so the engine would have reported
every night and acted on nothing, forever.

Fetching texts for all ~880 leads would turn a few dozen API calls into a
few thousand, and it is not necessary. The error only runs one way. A text
is a touch, so omitting texts can move a lead toward at risk and never away
from it — the leads flagged on the call-only signal are a superset of the
truly quiet ones. Checking just that superset, and reclassifying each lead
through the same combined list on the corrected touch, gives the same answer
a full text pull would for a few hundred calls instead of a few thousand.

The safety property is that the optimistic path cannot fail unsafely, only
fail back to doing nothing:

  - A lead whose text history could not be read is never acted on — held in
    the nudge loop as well as the sweep loop, since the nudge is what stamps
    customBattrAtRiskSince and arms the sweep.
  - Three consecutive lookup failures and the endpoint is written off for
    the run; every remaining candidate is held unread.
  - If nothing at all could be read, the blanket report-only hold from the
    previous commit applies exactly as before.
  - Leads held unread are dropped from the agent digests. An agent's list is
    better one lead short than one lead wrong; the omitted ones are named in
    the report's held-back section instead.

rules.textBackfill turns it off, and rules.maxTextChecksPerRun bounds a bad
night — candidates past the ceiling are held, not acted on, so lowering it
narrows what the engine may do.

The per-lead endpoint is not yet confirmed against the real API: personId is
named in FUB's own refusal message, which is good evidence and not proof.
inspect-fub-fields now probes it read-only and reports whether the scoped
read works and whether its rows carry a direction — one safe run settles it.

battr:test: 156 checks. The end-to-end scenario runs three ways — text
history served, refused in bulk but readable per lead, and unavailable by
any route.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BY8icdQLaS9G2YxbAmEBDv
