From 84ae9676985ead5804f5f1683e66e863652381f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 16:57:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/10] Pin which list id is which, so a transposition cannot
 pass silently
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hot Leads and Warm Back Up are near-mirrors — same stages, differing only on
lead age — but their thresholds are worlds apart. Hot Leads warns at 2 days
and sweeps at 4; Warm Back Up warns at 10 and sweeps at 13. Swap the two ids
and nothing errors: every list still behaves correctly, the wrong list just
answers to the wrong number. Ten thousand Warm Back Up leads would inherit a
4-day sweep line and the nightly run would empty the database into a pond.

The ids also key into observed.mjs, where Battr's own record counts live, so
a transposition there would silently change which list the engine is being
measured against.

No behavioural test can catch that, which is why five checks now pin the
pairing everywhere it is written down:

  - ids are unique
  - 1144 is Hot Leads, 1104 is Warm Back Up
  - each id carries the rules that belong to it — 1144 has the
    created-under-10-days filter and the 2-day warning, 1104 the
    created-over-10-days filter and the 10-day warning
  - lists.mjs and observed.mjs agree on both id and name
  - the record counts corroborate it: 1104 must dwarf 1144, since one is
    every early-pipeline lead older than ten days and the other is only the
    last ten days' worth (10,783 vs 19 on 2026-09-02)

No mapping was changed. census.mjs prints list.id and list.name off the same
object, so it cannot transpose anything on its own; the pairing comes
entirely from lists.mjs and is self-consistent today. These checks are a
tripwire, not a correction.

