Mike sent screenshots of all fifteen Aida List Summary panels from 3 Sep
2026. They are the first look we have had at what Battr actually selects,
rather than what we reverse-engineered from each list's compliance split.
Six of our rules were wrong. None of them could have caused a wrong sweep —
every corrected list is report-only, and the interlock gap made us stricter
about flagging, not looser about acting — but every one of them was
reporting on the wrong people.

MEMBERSHIP, corrected:

  YLOPO IMPORTANT  we had "source matches Ylopo". It is
                   tags CONTAINS ANY YPRIORITY, HANDRAISER, CALL_NOW=YES,
                   Y_SELLER_REPORT_ENGAGED, Y_SELLER_REPORT_VIEWED.
                   Thresholds 30/60, not the 7/10 we inferred.

  Zillow Important we had "source matches Zillow". It is
                   tags CONTAINS ANY Zillow High Intent Buyer / Seller /
                   Zillow High Intent, AND stage IS NONE OF the five
                   live-business stages, AND site activity inside 14 days.
                   Our 5/8 thresholds were right.

  Sphere & Past    we had five lead sources OR stage "Past Client". It is
    Clients        stage IS ANY OF Sphere, Closed. Thresholds 93/96, not 90/93.

  Current &        we had four stage names that do not exist in this account
    Upcoming       (Active Client, Pending, Current Client, Upcoming Client)
    Clients        and 14/30 as a placeholder. It is the seven real
                   live-business stages on a 10/13 clock.

  Active Leads     we had early-pipeline plus nurture. It is Attempted
                   Contact, Lead, Spoke with Customer, Sphere, Closed — no
                   Nurture, and it reaches into closed business.

Two of those four were source lists in our model and tag lists in Battr's.
That is not a detail: source says where a lead came from once, a tag says
the lead just raised a hand. We were reporting on the wrong population and
the counts were close enough not to look wrong.

THE WARN-FIRST INTERLOCK, restored:

Every nurture list and Warm Back Up have a Neglected tier of
"Last Communication Days Ago > N AND At Risk Notified Date Is Not Empty".
Ours had only the first half. No extra sweeps resulted — the sweep loop
enforces requireWarningBeforeSweep independently — but our Neglected counts
ran ahead of Battr's, and a reconciliation that disagrees for a known reason
is worth much less than one that agrees.

AI TEXT REPLIES is no longer a named gap: pond NONE and tags CONTAINS ANY
AI_ENGAGED, AI_NEEDS_FOLLOW_UP, 30/60, no actions. Added as list 1150,
report-only. Fourteen of the fifteen audits are now modelled; only Database
Health Score (a 13-source roll-up) still has no rule.

CLEAN UP: Nurtures No Timeframe is confirmed at >15 / >30 with every action
bucket empty, settling a contradiction between two of our own files about
whether those numbers had been read or inferred. They were read.

Also learned, and worth more than the corrections: the combined list's
exclusion reads "Agent's Assigned FUB Teams DOES NOT CONTAIN ANY Battr
Paused". A FUB TEAM on the assigned agent, not a Battr user group — a
first-class FUB concept that may well be readable from the API under a name
we have not tried. inspect-fub-fields now probes for it. Sweep confirmation
emails go to mike@therolandteam.com; audit alerts go to the agent only.

Baselines moved to the 3 Sep snapshot, which is complete for all fifteen.
Two observed-count assertions now check the shape rather than a literal — a
test that must be edited on every snapshot teaches people to edit tests.

battr:test: 172 checks. The corrections were caught BY the suite: sixteen
checks went red on the old rules, including "NO LIST IS SILENTLY EMPTY",
whose Sphere & Past Clients fixture stopped matching the moment the rule
became the real one.

