From 4e78cbb077a32ca4f02ecaea5f51bf97d91e6722 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 23:33:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] Record the overlap with Battr night by night, while
 there is still an overlap
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The team pays for Battr and runs this engine against the same database at the
same time. That is the only window in which this engine's answers can be
checked against a known-good one, and it closes when the subscription lapses.
After that every number it produces is taken on faith.

Until now the only comparison was against a frozen snapshot of Battr taken on
one day and hard-coded into observed.mjs. That catches a rule that is wrong
today; it cannot catch a rule that drifts next month, and it stops being worth
anything the moment either system changes.

battr-logs/comparison.csv is now a running record instead. The audit writes its
own row per list every night; Battr's rows are typed in from its Aida Audits
screen, which has no export. The nightly report gains a "Side by side with
Battr" section showing the drift, worst list first.

Design decisions worth keeping:

  - Plain CSV, quoted properly. It has to be editable by a person with a
    spreadsheet and no tooling, six months from now, after everyone involved
    has forgotten how any of this works. A list name containing a comma would
    otherwise shift every column right of it, silently.
  - A re-run replaces its own rows rather than appending, so running the audit
    twice in a day corrects the record instead of doubling it.
  - A list recorded on only one side is reported as unmatched, never dropped.
    A comparison that quietly discards what it cannot pair is how you end up
    believing two systems agree on everything you happened to record.
  - With no Battr numbers entered, the section says so. It does not report
    agreement it has not checked.
  - Sorted by drift, not by list id — a table ordered by id buries the one row
    that matters under fourteen that agree.
  - Recording is wrapped so a bookkeeping failure can never take down an audit.

Seeded with Battr's 3 Sep numbers for all eight actioned and report-only lists,
so the file ships with a worked example of the shape a person has to fill in,
and battr-logs/ gains a README explaining where each column comes from.

battr:test: 180 checks.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BY8icdQLaS9G2YxbAmEBDv
