From 96fc68db5c5044e62970d269f6f670238f0b9a39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 23:39:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] Make the undo brake reachable, and stop it lying about
 what it did
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two problems, both in the one piece of code that gets used on the worst
morning anyone will have with this system.

THE UNDO HAD NO DOOR. `--undo=<run-id>` existed in the engine and was printed
at the bottom of every report, but it was not a task in the workflow. The only
way to reach it was a laptop, a clone of this repo, and a copy of the FUB API
key. That is not an emergency brake. It is now a choice in the Run workflow
dropdown, with a field for the run id.

IT REPORTED SUCCESS IT DID NOT HAVE. The client short-circuits writes in dry
mode and returns {dry:true} rather than throwing, so an undo run without
BATTR_LIVE counted every lead as restored and printed "Restored 12/12 leads"
having touched nothing. A false all-clear is worse than a crash here: a crash
sends someone looking, an all-clear sends them to lunch while the leads sit in
the pond.

Undo now always writes. It reverses writes that already happened, so gating it
behind BATTR_LIVE was backwards — someone who switches the system off after a
bad night would find the undo switched off with it.

And the sharper one found while fixing that: undo would have happily "reversed"
a DRY RUN's log. Dry runs record the sweeps they would have made — that is what
makes a shadow run worth reading — but those leads were never moved. Undoing
them would have reassigned live leads to owners they were never taken from,
and the undo would have been the first thing all night to actually touch the
CRM. It now refuses, naming the run as a dry run.

A partial undo also now exits non-zero and names the leads still in the pond.
Half a reversal is a state someone has to know about, not a line in the middle
of a log nobody scrolls back through.

battr:test: 183 checks, including one that fails if the undo ever loses its
route out of the workflow again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BY8icdQLaS9G2YxbAmEBDv
